        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Product</strong>: Cmdr, a two-pane file manager. It's software for macOS 10.15 and later, on Apple
            silicon and Intel Macs. Each version has its own number, shown in Cmdr › About Cmdr. The app's bundle id is
            <code>com.veszelovszki.cmdr</code>, and its signature names Rymdskottkarra AB, Team ID
            <code>83H6YAQMNP</code> (<a href="#signing">signing</a>).
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>What it's for</strong>: browsing, searching, previewing, and managing files from the keyboard.
            That's copying, moving, renaming, deleting, comparing, and organizing files and folders on the Mac's own
            drives, and on servers, phones, and archives the user connects. It's for individuals and businesses, and it
            runs as the logged-in macOS user. It isn't made to serve files to other machines or to be built into
            another product. Its main security properties are in the <a href="#summary">summary</a> and under
            <a href="#permissions">Sandbox and permissions</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>When the risk goes up</strong>: Cmdr's defaults are the safest setup. These choices and situations
            raise the risk, and each one is up to the user or their IT team:
            <ul>
                <li>
                    <strong>Granting Full Disk Access</strong>: a flaw in Cmdr, or in a file format it reads, then
                    reaches every file on the Mac. Without it, macOS asks before Cmdr opens each protected folder.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Opening files from people you don't trust</strong>, like archives, PDFs, and images: Cmdr
                    reads them inside the app, which isn't sandboxed.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Connecting without encryption on a network you don't trust</strong>: a WebDAV or S3
                    server over plain <code>http://</code>, or an SMB server that doesn't encrypt, lets others on the
                    network read passwords and files on the way.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Turning on cloud AI</strong>: file names and, when asked, parts of files go to the AI
                    provider. Text inside a file can try to steer the assistant, which still can't change a file
                    without the user's approval.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Turning on the MCP server</strong>: AI tools on the same Mac that have its access token can
                    then control Cmdr.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Turning off updates</strong>, or holding Cmdr at a version: flaws that later releases fix
                    stay open on that Mac.
                </li>
                <li>
                    <strong>Using a Mac without FileVault</strong>: Cmdr's own data, like the index of file names and
                    the log of file operations, sits unencrypted on the disk.
                </li>
            </ul>
            What Cmdr doesn't do is under <a href="#gaps">Known limitations</a>, and the remaining risks, ranked, are
            in the
            <a href="https://github.com/vdavid/cmdr/blob/main/docs/threat-model.md#residual-risks-ranked">threat model</a
            >.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Support period</strong>: how long each version gets security fixes is under
            <a href="#report">Report a vulnerability</a> and in
            <a href="https://github.com/vdavid/cmdr/blob/main/SECURITY.md#supported-versions">SECURITY.md</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>EU declaration of conformity</strong>: the Act's main requirements apply from 2027-12-11. Cmdr's
            declaration of conformity will be published on this page once it's signed, before that date.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Software bills of materials (SBOMs)</strong>: attached to each release on GitHub
            (<a href="#signing">details</a>).
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Setting up Cmdr securely</strong>:
            <ul>
                <li>
                    Download Cmdr only from getcmdr.com, its
                    <a href="https://github.com/vdavid/cmdr/releases/latest">GitHub releases</a>, or the Homebrew
                    cask. To check a download, see "Build provenance" under <a href="#signing">signing</a>.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Keep the defaults unless you need something else: automatic updates on, AI and the MCP server off,
                    and nothing that other machines can connect to. Settings › Advanced › "Reset all to defaults"
                    brings them back.
                </li>
                <li>
                    Full Disk Access is optional. Grant it during setup to browse every folder without macOS asking
                    each time, or skip it.
                </li>
                <li>
                    For remote storage on a network you don't trust, pick SFTP, <code>https://</code>, or SMB with
                    encryption.
                </li>
                <li>Keep FileVault on.</li>
            </ul>
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>How updates install</strong>: by default, Cmdr checks for updates at launch and then every three
            hours. It downloads a new version, checks its signature, and installs it by itself, then asks you to
            restart. "Later" waits, and Cmdr asks again 24 hours later. Turning updates off is under
            <a href="#signing">signing</a>; managed Macs are under <a href="#mdm-deploy">deploying Cmdr</a>.
        </li>
        <li>
